Z||000006||Juiced_V8||10-17-2001||08:05 PM||||the fuel pump has a saftey shut off which is the oil psi sender when your oil psi drops too low the fuel pump shuts down automatically this also tells your fuel pump to shut off when you shut your car off  i killed 2 batterys before i figured it out on my 86 gt thats most likely your4 prob however i have a archie kitted v8 swap so i dont know where its going to be located stock..<P>------------------<BR>86 gt v8 nos fogger<BR>cant find it-cant afford it? make it yourself||||reg||12
Z||000007||Rodrv6||10-17-2001||09:58 PM||||Actually, that's a misconception.  The oil pressure switch on the Fiero will NOT shut off the pump if the oil pressure gets too low.  It does have a set of contacts that will run the pump if pressure is present, but it's wired in parallel to the fuel pump relay such that either the relay or the oil pressure switch will keep the pump running.  If the contacts in the pressure switch are stuck closed, the pump will not shut off.<BR>If you have a 4 cylinder, the oil pressure switch is screwed into the side of the block under the intake manifold.  On the V-6 it's near the top of the engine in the right front corner and is threaded into a tube.<P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  <BR>White 88 GT :)||||reg||1
Z||000008||maryjane||10-17-2001||10:43 PM||||If it's a misconception, it's a popular one. I had always believed just as you say, but in a thread a few weeks ago, I posted a diagram of the fuel system wiring. Wolfhound explained that one side of the wire run is for start,(the fuel pump relay), the other is for run(the oil press switch). I had a plugged oil pump screen back last spring and the oil light came on, but the eng would run. I still think the oil press switch will not shut off the eng, but I'm not going to drain my oil & run it to find out. Seems if the switch would kill the eng, you could disconect it (thereby making it an open circuit) and the eng would die from fuel starvation.<BR>Don<BR>  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Rodrv6:<BR><B>Actually, that's a misconception.  The oil pressure switch on the Fiero will NOT shut off the pump if the oil pressure gets too low.  It does have a set of contacts that will run the pump if pressure is present, but it's wired in parallel to the fuel pump relay such that either the relay or the oil pressure switch will keep the pump running.  If the contacts in the pressure switch are stuck closed, the pump will not shut off.<BR>If you have a 4 cylinder, the oil pressure switch is screwed into the side of the block under the intake manifold.  On the V-6 it's near the top of the engine in the right front corner and is threaded into a tube.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><p>[This message has been edited by maryjane (edited 10-17-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||Rodrv6||10-18-2001||06:05 AM||||If you unplug the oil pressure switch, the engine WILL continue to run (assuming the relay is working).  I installed a mechanical oil pressure gauge on mine for a while and totally removed the oil pressure switch.  I drove the car like that for over a year with no trouble.<P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  <BR>White 88 GT :)||||reg||1
Z||000010||2farnorth||10-18-2001||06:23 AM||||It is the oil pressure sending unit.  I found on my v6 that it is easier to get to from underneath the car (no a/c).  The unit looks like a medium sized odd shaped black light bulb.  It's on the front side of the engine and screws into the block at an angle.<P>If it's a 4 cyl, it will be on the back side of the engine near the distributor.  It will have a 3 wire connector attached to the end.<P>Good luck.<BR>Dave||||reg||1
Z||000011||TooManyFieros||10-18-2001||01:51 PM||||First, if you can read an electrical print, you will see that either the fuel pump relay or oil pressure switch will will energize the pump. If you read the manual (and listen to your pump) you will note that the pump quits (or should) three seconds after the key is placed in the on position (listen to this). Then, after three seconds, it's up to the oil pressure switch to take over. If you disconnect the oil pump sw. and the engine still runs, you have a stuck 3 second start relay (or the ECM is keeping it energized). <P>A stuck oil pressure switch will drain the battery. Been there and done that. A stuck relay will do the same. Pull them one at a time and see what kills the noise. <P><BR>Good luck  -  Jim<P><BR>[This message has been edited by TooManyFieros (edited 10-18-2001).]<p>[This message has been edited by TooManyFieros (edited 10-18-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||Rodrv6||10-18-2001||03:50 PM||||Still not quite right in the way the fuel pump relay and ECM work.  Yes, the pump will run for about 3 seconds when the key is turned on and then the ECM shuts off the pump relay if the engine is not being started.  But, if the engine is turning over, the ECM sees a reference pulse from the distributer and will close the fuel pump relay and keep it closed for the entire time the engine is running.  If the reference signal is not present, I.E. the engine is not running, then the relay is opened.  The oil pressure switch is just a backup to run the pump in case of a relay failure, nothing more.  This is straight out of the system description in the factory shop manuals.<P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  <BR>White 88 GT :)||||reg||1
Z||000013||TooManyFieros||10-19-2001||07:31 AM||||Well Rod, I stand corrected. Ya learn something new everyday. It's not true what they say, "you can't teach an old dog new tricks". You just taught this old dog something. Now if I would learn to read I wouldn't stick foot in mouth. Ha! I must learn not to do that. My shoe tastes bad.<BR>Jim||||reg||1
Z||000014||Rodrv6||10-19-2001||08:56 AM||||Jim, I know what you mean  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>I've had to taste my share of shoe leather over the years too!!||||reg||10
